      "prompt_tokens": 28,
      "completion_tokens": 1058,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Molecular Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Structure:** The molecule has a **bent (V) shape** (approx. 104.5° angle).\n*   **Polarity:** Because oxygen is much more electronegative than hydrogen, the electrons are pulled toward the oxygen. This gives the oxygen side a partial negative charge (δ−) and the hydrogen sides a partial positive charge (δ+). This makes water a **polar molecule**.\n\n### 2. The \"Secret Sauce\": Hydrogen Bonding\nBecause water is polar, the positive hydrogen end of one molecule attracts the negative oxygen end of a neighboring molecule. This attraction is called a **hydrogen bond**.\n*   While individually weak, collectively these bonds give water its unique, life-supporting properties.\n*   They cause water molecules to \"stick together\" (cohesion) and \"stick to other surfaces\" (adhesion).\n\n### 3. Unique Physical Properties (Caused by Hydrogen Bonding)\n| Property | Description | Why It Matters |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Universal Solvent** | Dissolves more substances than any other liquid. | Transports nutrients in blood, sap, and oceans; enables chemical reactions in cells. |\n| **High Specific Heat Capacity** | Absorbs a lot of heat before temperature rises. | Stabilizes Earth’s climate (oceans buffer temperature); prevents organisms from overheating. |\n| **High Heat of Vaporization** | Requires massive energy to turn liquid to gas. | Sweating/transpiration cools organisms efficiently. |\n| **Density Anomaly** | **Ice floats.** Water is densest at 4°C; expands when freezing. | Insulates lakes/oceans from the top down, allowing life to survive underneath in winter. |\n| **Surface Tension** | High cohesion creates a \"skin\" on the surface. | Allows insects to walk on water; helps pull water up plant roots (capillary action). |\n| **Three States on Earth** | Exists naturally as Solid (ice), Liquid (water), Gas (vapor) at Earth temperatures. | Drives the **Water Cycle** (evaporation, condensation, precipitation). |\n\n### 4. Biological Role\n*   **The Medium of Life:** All metabolic reactions (metabolism, photosynthesis, respiration) occur *in* water.\n*   **Transport:** Blood (animals) and sap (plants) are water-based transport systems for nutrients, oxygen, and waste.\n*   **Structure:** Provides turgor pressure in plant cells (keeps plants upright) and hydrostatic skeletons in worms/jellyfish.\n*   **Reactant:** A direct participant in hydrolysis (breaking bonds) and photosynthesis ($6CO_2 + 6H_2O \\rightarrow C_6H_{12}O_6 + 6O_2$).\n\n### 5. Planetary Context (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **Coverage:** ~71% of Earth's surface.\n*   **Distribution:**\n    *   **97.5% Salt Water** (Oceans).\n    *   **2.5% Fresh Water** (mostly locked in ice caps/glaciers/groundwater; <1% easily accessible in lakes/rivers).\n*   **Cycle:** The **Hydrologic Cycle** continuously moves water between reservoirs (ocean $\\rightarrow$ atmosphere $\\rightarrow$ land $\\rightarrow$ ocean) powered by the Sun.\n\n### 6. Human Use & Significance\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawal (irrigation).\n*   **Industry:** Cooling, processing, manufacturing (~19%).\n*   **Domestic:** Drinking, sanitation, hygiene (~11%).\n*   **Energy:** Hydroelectric power; cooling for thermal/nuclear plants.\n*   **Geopolitics:** A source of conflict and cooperation (transboundary rivers/aquifers).\n\n### Summary Definition\n> **Water is a small, polar molecule (H₂O) whose hydrogen bonding creates anomalous physical properties—specifically high heat capacity, surface tension, and a solid phase less dense than its liquid phase—making it the universal biological solvent and the primary regulator of Earth's climate and geology.**"
